23:17:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’ve nailed the crux of it: your definition of durability is *functional*—does the device still work?—and by that metric, plastic is unequivocally better. I can’t argue with that, because you’re right. A shattered glass screen can disable touch, make the display unreadable, let moisture in, and turn the phone into a paperweight instantly. A scratched plastic screen might look beat to hell, but as long as you can see the content and the touch works, it’s still a phone. So why did the industry abandon that functional toughness? The answer isn’t that they forgot about drops; it’s that the definition of “still working” changed when phones became all-glass touchscreen slabs. Here’s the shift that killed plastic for mainstream smartphones: **1. Display resolution and readability became central to function.** On an old Nokia with a low-res, monochrome screen, a scratch was a cosmetic blemish. You could still read the text because the information was simple. On a modern 450ppi touchscreen, a network of fine scratches diffuses the backlight, creates glare hotspots, and reduces contrast to the point where reading small text outdoors becomes genuinely difficult—this isn’t just “showroom-perfect,” it’s a functional degradation. A scratched plastic screen becomes a hazy, blurry mess over time, and since you’re now relying on reading tiny UI elements and HD photos, that degradation directly impacts usability. The function of the phone *as a display* is compromised. **2. Touch experience evolved from “good enough” to “expectedly seamless.”** Resistive touchscreens (the old pressure-based ones) didn’t care if the surface was scuffed. Capacitive touchscreens, which every modern phone uses, are more sensitive. Deep scratches on plastic don’t just look bad—they can catch your finger, create drag, and disrupt the smooth glide that gesture-based interfaces depend on. Over time, plastics can also turn slightly gummy as plasticizers break down, making swiping