the rare shattering event (mitigated by a case and screen protector) in exchange for a screen that stays clear and slick for years. The industry’s not unaware of this tension—modern “glass-tic” hybrids (ultra-thin glass with a plastic inner layer) are an attempt to get shatter resistance while keeping the hard scratch surface. But until someone makes an affordable, transparent plastic that’s as hard as glass, you’ll have to choose your poison: a screen that dies gracefully over years of scratches, or one that risks sudden death but stays pristine until that day. You’re simply choosing the former, and that’s a perfectly logical stance that the early cell phone era got right for functional toughness.
